OPNsense backup deletion and reversion protocol
===============================================

OPNsense core BackupController.deleteBackupAction($backup) and
revertBackupAction($backup) take the backup filename in the action URL path.
FreeSDN now sends POST /api/core/backup/deleteBackup/<filename> or
POST /api/core/backup/revertBackup/<filename>, with a valid empty JSON object.
The previous requests supplied the filename only in a JSON body.

The client and adapter share a filename check: one ASCII alphanumeric/dot/
underscore/hyphen segment, 1–128 characters, no double dot and not a single
dot. The 128-character bound matches the existing OPNsense API path guard.
Invalid names are rejected before transport. Longer vendor filenames may be
listed by the separate metadata API but are not actionable through this
bounded path contract.

Only the exact deleted or reverted status confirms the corresponding action.
not_found becomes a controlled not-found failure. Failed, missing, malformed,
opposite-action or otherwise unexpected outcomes cannot become success.
Successful data contains only the confirmed status and requested filename.
Transport/protocol failures return a fixed message that the operation could
not be confirmed; these adapter handlers do not log response bodies or raw
exception tracebacks. A timeout does not establish that the device did nothing.

Existing safety policy remains in force
---------------------------------------
The adapter still passes its existing direct_write_force flag, default false.
The client retains the original platform read-only gate, POST classification,
path guard and single-attempt write policy. This change does not enable writes
in a monitor-only deployment or automatically retry uncertain operations.
Cancellation propagates; it cannot undo a request already received by a device.
API permission, site, tenant and deleted-resource checks are unchanged.

Evidence and limits
-------------------
Regression checks use the actual client/adapter with controlled HTTPX transport
and actual PostgreSQL/API routes with synthetic identity. Independent checks
use incoming TCP HTTP plus a separate real TLS peer, synthetic Basic
Authentication, a fixture trust root and a controlled request deadline. The
peer checks method, filename path and JSON body, tests failures and cancellation,
and records actual request counts. The original cached-settings fixture error
is retained; the fixture now sets the loaded deployment default explicitly.
The real read-only gate and refusal controls remain exercised.

These results establish the tested protocol and error semantics, not actual
appliance restoration or reload behavior. No customer device is written by
the qualification fixtures. After a real revert, application/reload/reboot
requirements, connectivity recovery and functional validation remain a
separate controlled-hardware acceptance step. Current caller authorization
after I/O and server-side artifact identity locking are not introduced here.

Backup creation is unchanged and still needs review: the referenced upstream
controller has no matching backupAction for the currently configured create
endpoint. FreeSDN-managed artifact storage, retention, restore and measured
RPO/RTO remain separate incomplete capabilities. This correction does not
establish enterprise release readiness.

